<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Aka

//aka// — Hawaiian: image, reflection, likeness; to be like. The English //also-known-as// happens to
share the same three letters, and it happens to share LIKENESS too — a happy accident this root leans
on, never a borrowing.

''One family, one axis.'' `aka` and `kahea` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kahea`) sugar the SAME
`pranala family=transclusion` — one edge family, split on PROPAGATION alone: `kahea` holds the
push-forward pole, a live call; `aka` holds the pinned pole, a PIN. `aka` pins a `lar:` target, full
stop — where the `aka` sits in a carrier decides nothing about what it does. Rendering follows the
TARGET instead.

''Informative, not binding.'' `aka` pairs with `kanawai` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kanawai`),
a second pin over the same family that splits from `aka` on FORCE rather than propagation: `aka`
cites informatively — naming a work, a cross-reference, a likeness the carrier does not bind itself
to; `kanawai` declares a BINDING relation — the law a carrier holds itself to. A carrier's own
normative verbs bind under `kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119"`; a carrier merely citing a
related document, or mirroring its own English alias against a Hawaiian canonical, stays `aka`.

''A citation, when the target names a reference.'' Pinning a reference meme
(`reference-kind` set — an RFC, a standard, a cited work) renders as a citation: the work's own
bibliographic facts, never an inlined body.

''A frozen image, when the target carries content.'' Pinning a content meme or slot instead embeds a
frozen image of it: its bytes pinned at the moment the pin resolves, read-only, no ownership stake.

A TW5 shadow tiddler names something else entirely, and the resemblance misleads: a shadow tiddler
names a LIVE fallback layer, visible only while nothing overrides it — the opposite posture from a
frozen pin, which never yields to an override and never falls back. `aka` pins once, at the moment the
pin resolves, and holds that pin until the carrier re-weaves.

Sugar for `<<~ pranala from=? -> to=URI family=transclusion>>`. The `transclusion` family is confidence-bounded:
a frozen embed can age from its source, so edges SHOULD carry a confidence rating when the embed is uncertain.

''English weave name: `pin`.'' `aka` weaves in English as `pin`, its own weave name, not a mirror of
any other sigil — `pin` names the posture directly, whichever rendering the target calls for.
`snapshot` and `shadow` retire from the grammar; canon names neither as live vocabulary.

''Propagation split.'' `aka` and `kahea` sugar the same `transclusion` family from opposite
propagation postures, and each links the other directly. `kahea` weaves ''live'' — as an include,
re-resolving the target on every render pass. `aka` weaves ''frozen'' — by pinning the target at the
moment the weave runs, so the source carries a pin rather than a subscription. Neither sigil mirrors
the other: `aka` names its own sigil, its own family-contract row (`propagation:none` against
`kahea`'s `propagation:push-forward`), and its own English weave name.

''Weave slice, built.'' A frozen read renders through each child-slot sigil's `~aka~<sigil>`
procedure and its AkaTemplate cascade (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/render-pipeline#/render-posture`); the general
inline-weave slice that stands `aka` up as a first-class content-substitution pass
stands built — `weaveAka` (weave/index.ts) pins the target's
current text, pinned with the target's own `ni:` check, whole-carrier or `#/slot`-scoped.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ sense the target URI — this meme observes that one; note whether confidence bound is needed
⏿ orient: family:transclusion, traversal:source-to-target, propagation:none; read-only; no execution pulse
◇ confidence-bounded: if the observation carries uncertainty, SHOULD declare confidence on the edge
▶ emit EdgeSugarNode with sigil:aka, family:transclusion; render the pinned likeness/frozen image at source position
↺ the pin reads the target as it stood at the moment the weave pinned it; source holds no lifecycle stake in target; confirm the pinned image stable; target still resolvable; no ownership or mutation transferred

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Law (Kānāwai)

An aka MUST bind a source socket and a target URI.
An aka MUST carry `family:transclusion`.
An aka MUST leave the target unchanged.
An aka MUST stay inert, carrying no execution pulse.
An aka SHOULD carry `confidence` when the observation is uncertain.
An aka MUST stay acyclic — directly and transitively.

`family:transclusion` — frozen content embedding; the source shows the target's content through
without consuming or owning it. Propagation is none: a frozen embed is pulled, not pushed.

The `transclusion` family is `confidence-bounded: true`. This means the parser flags aka edges
that lack a confidence rating when the source-side context signals uncertainty.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/syntax>>

!! Syntax

```text
<<~ aka URI>>
<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/meme">>
<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-4151">>
```

`?` source resolves to the nearest enclosing socket.
Fragment `#slot-name` addresses a named ahu child directly.

Regex (canonical):
```
/<<~\s*aka\s+(\S+)\s*>>/
```
Groups: `[full, target-uri]`

Full pranala expansion:
```text
<<~ pranala from="?" -> to="URI" family="transclusion" lifecycle="instance" traversal="source-to-target" propagation="none">>
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/schema>>

!! Schema (machine-readable)

Canonical TOML form. Source of truth for `BUILTIN_AKA_RE` and `akaDefaultFamily`
in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/pranala-parser.ts`.

```toml
sigil          = "aka"
kind           = "edge-sugar"
layer          = "both"
default-family = "transclusion"
render-mode    = null
alias          = []

pattern = '<<~\s*aka\s+(\S+)\s*>>'

[captures]
to = 1

[family-contract]
role-recommended   = false
confidence-bounded = true
```

